{0 The checker and Quint}

The checker ([silica.check], module {!Check}) runs a component in every state
that it can reach. In each state it renders the view, finds every event that
can happen, and checks rules of the state, the view and the step. It searches
breadth first, so each finding comes with a shortest list of steps that leads
to it. It goes on after a finding and reports all of them.

States and events must be plain data: the checker compares and hashes them.
A function in a state or an event raises {!Check.Invalid_check}.

{1:component Check a component}

{!Check.component} takes a component and gives a check, a value with the
samples, inputs, bounds, properties and allowances. {!Check.run} runs it and
gives a {!Check.type-report}. The events of a state are:

- the handlers of the view: press, double press, submit, focus, blur, pointer
  enter and leave, and the key handlers with each key of [keys];
- the events of the held sources of the state, such as the keys of a
  [Source.keys] table or the events of a dismiss layer;
- the events of running timers, in any order, as time is not counted;
- the answers of running calls and the states of running subscriptions (see
  {{!section-samples}Samples});
- [inputs state]: the events that need a value, such as the text of an
  [on_input];
- every value in [props], given as new props ([Check.Props p]).

A machine made with [Machine.make] has the props [()]. A machine with props
needs [props]: the check starts once with each of them. The input of a step is
a {!Check.type-input}: [Check.Event e] or [Check.Props p].

A handler on an element with [disabled] is not an event. [within] limits a
state space that has no end: the checker does not explore a state outside it.
[max_states] (100 000) stops a search that is too big; the report is then not
[complete].

{@ocaml[
# let report =
    Check.run
      (Check.component
         ~inputs:(fun _ -> [ Doc_debounce.Typed "a"; Doc_debounce.Typed "" ])
         Doc_debounce.component)
  in
  (Check.passed report, report.states, report.transitions);;
- : bool * int * int = (true, 3, 9)
]}

{!Check.passed} is true when the report has no violation, no unreached state,
and is [complete].

{1:rules Rules}

The built-in rules hold for every component. {!Check.val-rules} lists each with
its id, its message and its hint:

- [unique-refs]: refs are unique in a view.
- [focus-target]: a [Focus], [Blur], [Scroll_into_view] or [Scroll_to] action
  names a ref of the next view.
- [boundary-reset]: the fallback of each boundary can reset it.
- [dead-end]: some event can happen, unless the state is final or the view
  holds a child component or an island.
- [timer-owner], [call-owner]: every running timer, call and subscription
  belongs to the current state. A finding is a bug of Silica.
- [ignored-event]: the machine handles every event of a handler of the view,
  of a timer, of a call answer and of a subscription. It may ignore the
  events of held sources, of [inputs], of action results and new props. So an
  update that ends with [| _ -> Ignore] cannot hide a button that does
  nothing.
- [raised]: the update, the view, a handler or a property raised an
  exception. The search does not go on from that state.
- [unreached-state]: a name of the [states] of the machine (see
  {!Silica.Machine.val-shape}) that no step reached.
- [state-limit]: the search stopped at [max_states].
- [live-uses-client-source], [live-uses-client-action]: see
  {{!section-live}Live components}.
- [route-round-trip], [link-parses]: see {{!section-app}Routed apps}.

{2:accessibility Accessibility rules}

These rules read the view of each state as data, with the roles and names of
{!Silica.Accessibility}. They see no browser and no CSS, and they skip hidden
elements. A component is checked alone: they do not see the view of its parent,
and they see a child component only as a node.

- [accessible-name]: a button, link, checkbox, radio, switch, tab, menu item,
  option or tree item, and each element with a press handler, has a name. It
  cannot see a name that CSS paints, such as an icon font.
- [field-label]: a field ([input], [textarea], [select], or a textbox,
  searchbox, combobox, listbox, slider or spinbutton role) has a name: a
  [<label for>], a [<label>] around it, [aria-label], [aria-labelledby],
  [title] or [placeholder].
- [img-alt]: an [img] has [alt] ([alt=""] for decoration), a name, or the role
  [none]. It does not check that the text is useful, and does not look at
  [svg].
- [unique-ids]: an id is on one element of the view. It cannot see the ids of
  the parent, nor of another instance of the same component.
- [aria-reference]: [aria-labelledby], [aria-describedby],
  [aria-activedescendant] and the [for] of a [label] name ids of the view. The
  rule is off for a view that holds a child component or a chunk that is not
  loaded, and it does not check [aria-controls] and [aria-owns], because their
  target is often in the parent, as the panel of a tab is.
- [valid-role]: a [role] has an ARIA 1.2 role ([doc-] and [graphics-] roles
  count), and an explicit role has the attribute it needs: [aria-checked] for
  checkbox, radio, switch, menuitemcheckbox and menuitemradio (a native
  checkbox has it), [aria-level] for a heading that is not [h1] to [h6],
  [aria-expanded] for combobox, [aria-valuenow] for slider.
- [required-children]: a listbox holds only options, a tablist only tabs, a
  menu or menubar only menu items, through elements with no role and groups.
  Text in them is a finding; an empty one is not. It does not follow
  [aria-owns].
- [nested-interactive]: a button, link, tab, option, checkbox, radio, switch,
  menuitemcheckbox or menuitemradio does not hold a control or an element with
  a [tabindex] of 0 or more.
- [keyboard-access]: an element with a press handler is a [button], a link, a
  field or [summary], or has a [tabindex]; a press handler on an element with
  no role, or with the role button, link, checkbox or switch, and no
  [tabindex] is a finding. An option or a menu item with no [tabindex] is not,
  because its widget can move focus with [aria-activedescendant]. The rule
  does not see a key handler on an element above.
- [dialog-name]: a dialog or alert dialog has a name.
- [dialog-focus]: when the view shows a dialog with [aria-modal="true"], the
  state owns a {!Silica.Source.focus_trap} on it or on an element around it.
  It cannot see a native [<dialog>] opened with [showModal].
- [heading-order]: a heading is at most one level below the heading before it;
  each portal starts again. The first heading of a view can have any level,
  because the rule cannot see the headings of the page around a component.
- [name-spacing]: in the name that a button, link, tab, option, menu item,
  checkbox, radio, switch, tree item or heading takes from its content, two
  inline elements (such as [span]) or an element and a text do not join a
  letter or digit to a letter or digit with no space, as
  [<a><span>ENG-2</span><span>Second</span></a>] gives ["ENG-2Second"]. A block
  element ([div], [p], [li]) counts as a space. It cannot see CSS: a [span]
  that CSS makes a block or a flex item can show a space in a browser, but the
  view still has none, so add a [" "] text node or an [aria-label].

Each violation in the report is a record: the [rule], a [message] with the
state, the element and the event, a [hint] that says how to fix it, the
[counterexample] (the steps), the [state] and its [state_name] at the failing
point, the [view] of that state as text ({!Silica.View_text}), and the
[element] for a rule of the view. Each step has its [input], the name of its
[event], its [cause] (the element and handler of the view, the timer, the
call, the source), and the names of the states [from] and [target].

When the machine ignores the event of a button, disable the button (for
example with {!Silica.Machine.can}), or handle the event, for example with
[Stay []]. This lamp ignores [Toggle] when it is on. The trace ends with the
ignored event:

{@ocaml[
# let broken =
    let machine =
      Silica.Machine.make ~id:"broken-lamp" ~init:Doc_lamp.Off
        ~shape:Doc_lamp.state_shape (fun state Doc_lamp.Toggle ->
          match state with
          | Doc_lamp.Off -> Silica.Machine.Go Doc_lamp.On
          | Doc_lamp.On -> Silica.Machine.Ignore)
    in
    Silica.Component.make machine Doc_lamp.view;;
val broken : (unit, Doc_lamp.state, Doc_lamp.event) Silica.Node.component =
  {Silica.Component.machine = <abstr>; view = <fun>}
# Format.printf "%a@." Check.pp_report (Check.run (Check.component broken));;
broken-lamp: 2 states, 2 transitions
violation 1: ignored-event
  In state on, the view offers press on button "Turn off" at div > button gives the event (unnamed), and the update returns Ignore.
  hint: Handle (unnamed) in state on, or do not offer it there: disable the element with Machine.can, or leave its timer or call out of owns of on.
  1. off -> on: (unnamed event), by the view offers press on button "Turn on" at div > button
  2. on -> on: (unnamed event), by the view offers press on button "Turn off" at div > button
  state: on
  view:
    text "The lamp is on."
    button "Turn off" [press]
- : unit = ()
]}

This machine has no [events] shape, so its events have no name; give it one
(see {!page-machines.events}) and the steps name them.

{1:properties Properties}

A property of the app is a name and a condition that must hold in every state
that the search reaches. {!Check.always} makes one; a condition reads the
state ({!Check.val-state}, {!Check.state_named}), the view ({!Check.val-view}), both
({!Check.holds}), or the event that reached the state ({!Check.val-event}), and
combines with [&&&], [|||] and [not_]. A property can give its own [hint];
[{state}] in it is the name of the failing state. This one fails on the
stepper of {!page-components}:

{@ocaml[
# let at_most_two =
    Check.always ~name:"at-most-two"
      ~hint:"Stop the count at two in {state}."
      (Check.state (fun (Doc_stepper.Count count) -> count <= 2));;
val at_most_two : (Doc_stepper.state, '_weak1) Check.property = <abstr>
# let report =
    Check.run
      (Check.component ~props:[ "Adults" ] ~properties:[ at_most_two ]
         ~within:(fun (Doc_stepper.Count count) -> count <= 5)
         Doc_stepper.component)
  in
  List.iter
    (fun (violation : _ Check.violation) ->
      Printf.printf "%s: %s\n%s\n" violation.rule violation.hint
        (String.concat ", "
           (List.map (fun (step : _ Check.step) -> step.event)
              (Check.steps violation.counterexample))))
    report.violations;;
at-most-two: Stop the count at two in count.
up, up, up
- : unit = ()
]}

The search keeps the first violation of each rule, state name, element and
message, so the list stays short. [max_violations] (50) stops the search when
the list is full; the report is then [truncated].

{1:allow Allowances}

A rule that does not apply to a state is turned off with an allowance and a
reason: {!Check.allow}[ ~rule ?state ?element ~reason ()]. The report lists
each allowance and how many violations it turned off, so a reviewer sees what
was turned off and why. A state that no run can reach on purpose is allowed
with [~rule:"unreached-state" ~state].

{1:report The report}

Besides the violations, the report gives the counts of [states] and
[transitions], the [unreached] states, the [spec] that the run found (see
{!section-quint}), and for each state name in the order the search reached it:
[paths], a shortest list of steps from the start, and [views], the view of its
first state as text.

{@ocaml[
# let report = Check.run (Check.component Doc_lamp.component) in
  List.iter
    (fun (name, steps) ->
      Printf.printf "%s: [%s]\n" name
        (String.concat ", " (List.map (fun (step : _ Check.step) -> step.event) steps)))
    report.paths;;
off: []
on: [toggle]
- : unit = ()
# let report = Check.run (Check.component Doc_lamp.component) in
  List.iter (fun (name, view) -> Printf.printf "%s:\n%s" name view) report.views;;
off:
text "The lamp is off."
button "Turn on" [press]
on:
text "The lamp is on."
button "Turn off" [press]
- : unit = ()
]}

{1:samples Samples}

The checker has no server. A sample gives the values that the server could
give:

- {!Check.answers} [endpoint values]: a call can end with each value, and
  also with [Failed (Http 500)] and [Failed (Network "down")]. [rejected]
  gives the application errors that it can end with, as [Rejected x].
- {!Check.val-states} [topic values]: a subscription gets [Loading None], then up
  to [arrivals] states, one loss of the connection with the old data, and one
  fatal failure.

An endpoint with no sample can only fail. So the error paths are always
checked.

{@ocaml[
# let report =
    Check.run
      (Check.component
         ~samples:[ Check.answers Tutorial_api.greeting [ "Hello, Ada" ] ]
         Doc_hello.component)
  in
  (report.states, List.length (List.hd report.specs).edges);;
- : int * int = (3, 3)
]}

{1:children Children}

The checker does not run a child component inside the check of its parent. A
report lists each child in [not_explored], and keeps the props that the
parent gave in [children]. Check the child alone with those props:

{@ocaml[
# let children =
    let report = Check.run (Check.component Doc_party.component) in
    print_endline (String.concat ", " report.not_explored);
    report.children;;
component stepper
val children : Check.children = <abstr>
# let props = Check.props children Doc_stepper.component;;
val props : string list = ["Adults"; "Children"]
# (Check.run
     (Check.component ~props
        ~within:(fun (Doc_stepper.Count count) -> count <= 3)
        Doc_stepper.component)).states;;
- : int = 8
]}

{1:live Live components}

{!Check.live} checks a Live component. Its machine runs on the server, so a
state that owns a source of the page (a focus trap, a key table, a dismiss
layer) is a violation of [live-uses-client-source], and an action that needs
an answer of the page ([Measure], [Copy], [Load_script]) is a violation of
[live-uses-client-action].

{1:app Routed apps}

{!Check.app} explores the root of a router app with a simulated history: link
presses, back, forward and the navigation actions. [routes] lists route
values to check: for a route with a query, give one value with the default and
one without. [inputs] gives the URLs that a user can type. Two more rules
hold:

- [route-round-trip]: each route reads back from its own URL.
- [link-parses]: the [href] of each link in a view prints back to the same
  URL, and does not go to the not-found route.

{@ocaml[
# let report =
    let open Doc_books in
    Check.run
      (Check.app
         ~inputs:(fun _ ->
           List.map
             (fun path -> Silica.Router.Url_changed (Silica.Url.of_string path))
             [ "/"; "/books/7"; "/search?q=ocaml"; "/nowhere" ])
         ~routes:
           [
             Home;
             Book 7;
             Search { text = ""; page = 1 };
             Search { text = "ocaml"; page = 2 };
             Not_found "/x";
           ]
         ~parse:(Silica.Router.parse routes) ~print:(Silica.Router.print routes)
         ~is_not_found:(Silica.Router.is_not_found routes)
         ~url_event:(fun url -> Silica.Router.Url_changed url)
         app)
  in
  (Check.passed report, report.states);;
- : bool * int = (true, 1696)
]}

The pages of this example are plain nodes, so they are part of the check. A
page that is a component node is listed in [not_explored]: check it alone,
as a child.

{1:quint Diagrams and Quint}

{!Silica.Spec} is the shape of a machine that a checker run found: the states it
reached, the timers and sources of each, and the steps it took with the names
of their events (see {!page-machines.spec}). The report gives it in [specs].
The names come from the [events] of the machine (see {!page-machines.events});
a machine with no [events] gives empty names. The states come in a fixed
order: the initial state first, then by name. It has these exports:

- {!Silica.Spec.to_text}: a text form to read and diff, which
  {!Silica.Spec.of_text} reads back.
- {!Silica.Spec.to_dot}: a Graphviz diagram; {!Silica.Spec.to_mermaid}: a
  Mermaid diagram.
- {!Silica.Spec.to_quint}: a module for {{:https://quint-lang.org} Quint}, a
  language for the specification of systems. The module has the state, the
  running timers and sources, and the last event. It has one transition
  for each edge and event, written as a Quint [action], and the invariants
  [resources_owned] (every running resource belongs to the current state),
  [can_move] and [inv] (both).

{@ocaml[
# let report = Check.run (Check.component Doc_lamp.component) in
  print_string (Silica.Spec.to_quint (List.hd report.specs));;
// Generated from the spec of the machine "lamp". Do not edit.
module lamp {
  type State = | Off | On
  type Event = OnStart | OnAny | OnToggle

  var state: State
  var running: Set[str]
  var last: Event

  pure def owned(s: State): Set[str] = match s {
    | Off => Set()
    | On => Set()
  }

  pure val finals: Set[State] = Set()
  pure val movable: Set[State] = Set(Off, On)

  action enter(s: State, e: Event): bool = all {
    state' = s,
    running' = owned(s),
    last' = e,
  }

  action init = all {
    state' = Off,
    running' = owned(Off),
    last' = OnStart,
  }

  action off_to_on_on_toggle = all { state == Off, enter(On, OnToggle) }
  action on_to_off_on_toggle = all { state == On, enter(Off, OnToggle) }

  action step = any {
    off_to_on_on_toggle,
    on_to_off_on_toggle,
  }

  val resources_owned = running.subseteq(owned(state))
  val can_move = finals.contains(state) or movable.contains(state)
  val inv = resources_owned and can_move
}
- : unit = ()
]}

The model holds the edges only, not the code of the update, so it allows more
runs than the machine. It holds only the edges that the run took: give the
checker the samples and inputs that reach every state.

The repository writes the exports of its machines in a dune rule, and keeps a
copy in [spec/] next to the tests. A [diff] rule fails the build when a change
of a machine changes its spec, so a change of the shape is always seen in a
review:

From [test/example_check/dune]:
{@dune skip[
(rule
 (alias spec)
 (action
  (diff spec/router.qnt router.qnt)))
]}

[test/quint/check.ts] runs Quint on each module: it type checks it and runs
random traces against the invariant [inv]. A module with a seeded bug must
fail:

From [test/quint/check.ts]:
{@ts skip[
const runArgs = (file: string) => [
  "run", file, "--invariant=inv", "--max-steps=20", "--max-samples=500", "--seed=1", "--backend=typescript",
];
]}
